  "step_3": "The decision reason requires a comparison based solely on the probability of the minimum outcome, favoring the lottery with the higher such probability. In this case, both Lottery B’s minimum outcome (3000 Euros) and its 100% probability are higher than Lottery A’s 0 Euros at 20%, so the reason would favor B. The individual also ends up valuing the certainty of Lottery B and explicitly mentions the 20% risk of nothing, which aligns with attention to the minimum outcome’s likelihood. However, their justification is not based on a rule about minimum outcomes per se, but on personal risk aversion and the subjective sufficiency of 3000 Euros. They do not indicate a general preference for higher probability of the minimum outcome as a decision rule, but rather an emotional or personal evaluation of risk and need. While the considered information partially overlaps (awareness of 20% chance of nothing), the justification is more affective and subjective than rule-based. Therefore, it is plausible but not certain that the decision reason was used as a formal rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 60
